“Lent: Giving Up or Making Space?”

[Soft ambient music fades in — slow, spacious, reflective]

When most people think of Lent, they think of giving something up.
Chocolate. Social media. Snacks.
Little comforts that make life feel easier.

But Lent… is not really about losing something.
Lent is about making space.

Making space for God.
Making space for clarity.
Making space for the parts of your soul you’ve been too busy to notice.

If you sit with the tradition long enough, you begin to realize something:
The Church never asks us to give things up just to prove we can.
The goal is not deprivation.
The goal is freedom.

Because every one of us carries things — habits, patterns, distractions —
that slowly fill the inner rooms of our hearts.
And over time, without even noticing,
we run out of space for God to speak.

Lent is the season where God gently says:
“Let Me in.
Make room for Me.
Let Me reorder what matters.”

So yes, fasting is part of Lent.
But fasting is not the destination.
Fasting is the doorway.

When you fast from noise, you make space for clarity.
When you fast from distraction, you make space for prayer.
When you fast from comfort, you make space for courage.
When you fast from habits that numb you, you make space for healing.

Lent is not a season of shrinking.
It’s a season of opening.

[Soft pause — music breathes]

Think about your life for a moment.
Think about your routines, your habits, your rhythms.
Think about the things you reach for when you’re tired, overwhelmed, or lonely.

Some of those things are harmless.
Some of them are helpful.
But some of them… quietly take up space that belongs to God.

Lent invites you to notice.
Not with shame.
Not with guilt.
But with honesty.

Because God doesn’t want your perfection.
He wants your attention.

He wants the space where He can meet you.
Where He can speak into your life.
Where He can breathe peace into the places that feel tight or crowded.

[Music deepens slightly]

So this year, instead of asking,
“What should I give up?”
Maybe ask a different question:

“Where do I need more space for God?”

Do you need space in your schedule?
Space in your mind?
Space in your habits?
Space in your heart?

Maybe the thing you give up is simply the thing that’s been filling the silence.
Maybe the thing you let go of is the thing that’s been drowning out God’s whisper.
Maybe the thing you release is the thing that’s been keeping you from rest.

Lent is not about punishing yourself.
It’s about returning to yourself.
Returning to the person God created you to be.
Returning to the peace you were made for.

[Soft pause]

And here’s the beautiful part:
When you make space for God…
He fills it gently.

Not with pressure.
Not with demands.
But with presence.

With clarity.
With healing.
With a sense of being held.

Lent is the season where we clear the clutter —
not to live with less,
but to live with more of what matters.

More peace.
More purpose.
More God.

So as you enter this season, remember:
You’re not just giving something up.
You’re opening something up.
You’re making room.
You’re creating space.
You’re preparing your heart for the One who has been waiting for you all along.

[Music swells gently, then fades]

This Lent…
Don’t just give something up.
Make space.
And let God fill it.
